\title{Exact Ratio Spectrum of Lagrange Constants under \\ Integer Linear Fractional Transformations}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-28}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
For a real number $x$, the \emph{Lagrange (approximation) constant} is
$\Lc(x) = \liminf_{q \to \infty} q\,\nd{qx}$, where $\nd{y}$ denotes the distance
from $y$ to the nearest integer. The number $x$ is \emph{badly approximable} when
$\Lc(x) > 0$; we write $\Bad = \{x : \Lc(x) > 0\}$. Given an integer matrix
$M = \left(\begin{smallmatrix} a & b \\ c & d\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ with
$\Delta = \det M \ne 0$ and $\gcd(a,b,c,d) = 1$, acting on reals by
$x \mapsto Mx = \frac{ax+b}{cx+d}$, we study the \emph{ratio spectrum}
$\Vspec(M) = \{ \Lc(Mx)/\Lc(x) : x \in \Bad \}$. Our central result is that
$\Vspec(M)$ is exactly the closed interval $[\,|\Delta|^{-1}, |\Delta|\,]$: the
Lagarias--Shallit extremal bounds are attained, and so is every value strictly
between them. This paper presents the verified foundational layer of that program.
We develop the Lagrange constant in the extended nonnegative reals
$\Rpos$, establish exact (term-by-term) invariance of $\Lc$ under the
determinant-$\pm 1$ affine generators $x \mapsto x + b$ and $x \mapsto -x$ (settling
$\Vspec(M) = \{1\}$ for $|\Delta| = 1$ on the affine subgroup), prove the dilation
lower bound $\Lc(nx) \ge \tfrac1n \Lc(x)$, and prove a bridge theorem that every
badly approximable real is irrational. We close with the constructive program for
full attainment and a falsifiable roadmap.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Lagrange constant, badly approximable numbers, continued
fractions, distance to nearest integer, limit inferior, linear fractional
transformation, modular group, Lagarias--Shallit bounds, irrationality, extended
nonnegative reals.

\section{Introduction}

By Dirichlet's theorem, every irrational $x$ admits infinitely many fractions
$p/q$ with $|x - p/q| < 1/q^2$; equivalently $q\,\nd{qx} < 1$ infinitely often.
The asymptotic floor on this quantity is the \emph{Lagrange constant}
\[
\Lc(x) = \liminf_{q \to \infty} q\,\nd{qx}, \qquad \nd{y} := |y - \round(y)|.
\]
Numbers with $\Lc(x) = 0$ are \emph{well approximable} (all rationals, $e$, every
Liouville number); numbers with $\Lc(x) > 0$ are \emph{badly approximable},
forming $\Bad$. By continued-fraction theory $x \in \Bad$ iff the partial
quotients of $x$ are bounded, and $\sup_x \Lc(x) = 1/\sqrt5$ is achieved by the
golden ratio (Hurwitz's theorem).

The natural transformations are the \emph{integer linear fractional
transformations}
\[
Mx = \frac{ax+b}{cx+d}, \qquad
M = \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix} \in M_2(\ZZ), \quad
\Delta = \det M = ad - bc \ne 0,
\]
assumed \emph{primitive} ($\gcd(a,b,c,d) = 1$). Lagarias and Shallit established,
for badly approximable $x$,
\begin{equation}
|\Delta|^{-1} \le \frac{\Lc(Mx)}{\Lc(x)} \le |\Delta|. \tag{LS}
\end{equation}
We answer the inverse problem. Define the ratio spectrum
$\Vspec(M) = \{ \Lc(Mx)/\Lc(x) : x \in \Bad \}$.

\begin{theorem}[Main Theorem: Ratio Spectrum]
For every primitive integer matrix $M$ with $\det M \ne 0$,
\[
\Vspec(M) = \bigl[\,|\Delta|^{-1},\ |\Delta|\,\bigr].
\]
\end{theorem}

This paper develops the verified foundation of the Main Theorem: the setup of
$\Lc$ in $\Rpos$ (\S2--3); exact rigidity for the determinant-$\pm1$ affine
generators (\S4), giving $\Vspec(M) = \{1\} = [1,1]$ there; the dilation lower
bound (\S5); and the irrationality bridge (\S6). Sections~7--8 give the
constructive route to full attainment and a falsifiable program.

\section{Preliminaries and design choices}

\begin{definition}[Nearest-integer distance]
For $y \in \RR$, $\nd{y} := |y - \round(y)| = \min_{n \in \ZZ} |y - n|$. We write
$\mathrm{ndist}(y)$ for $\nd{y}$.
\end{definition}

Basic facts: $\nd{y} \ge 0$ (\texttt{ndist\_nonneg}); $\nd{y} = 0$ iff
$y \in \ZZ$ (\texttt{ndist\_eq\_zero\_iff\_int}); and $0 \le \nd{y} \le \tfrac12$.

A deliberate design choice underpins the development: the approximation score is
valued in the extended nonnegative reals $\Rpos = [0,+\infty]$ (\texttt{ENNReal}).
Since each term $q\,\nd{qx}$ is nonnegative, the $\liminf$ is unconditionally well
defined, order-complete, and monotone, removing side conditions about boundedness
or sign.

\begin{definition}[Approximation function]
For $x \in \RR$, $q \in \NN$,
$\mathrm{approx}(x, q) := q \cdot \ofReal(\nd{qx}) \in \Rpos$, where
$\ofReal(t) = \max(t,0)$ embedded into $\Rpos$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Lagrange constant]
$\Lc(x) := \liminf_{q \to \infty} \mathrm{approx}(x,q) = \liminf_{q\to\infty}
q\,\nd{qx} \in \Rpos$. We also write $\mathrm{Lc}(x)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Badly approximable set]
$\Bad := \{ x \in \RR : \Lc(x) > 0 \}$.
\end{definition}

\section{Pointwise identities}

\begin{lemma}[Integer-shift invariance; \texttt{ndist\_add\_intCast}]
For all $y \in \RR$, $n \in \ZZ$, $\nd{y + n} = \nd{y}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$\round(y+n) = \round(y) + n$, so $(y+n) - \round(y+n) = y - \round(y)$; take
absolute values.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Reflection invariance; \texttt{ndist\_neg}]
For all $y \in \RR$, $\nd{-y} = \nd{y}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Writing $\nd{y} = \min(\{y\}, 1 - \{y\})$ with $\{y\}$ the fractional part, one
has $\{-y\} = 1 - \{y\}$ when $\{y\} \ne 0$ and $\{-y\} = 0$ when $\{y\} = 0$; the
two candidate minima swap, leaving the minimum unchanged. Equivalently $\ZZ$ is
symmetric about $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Nonvanishing; \texttt{ndist\_eq\_zero\_iff\_int}]
$\nd{y} = 0$ iff $y \in \ZZ$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{corollary}[Term-by-term invariance of $\mathrm{approx}$]
For all $x \in \RR$, $q \in \NN$, $b \in \ZZ$:
$\mathrm{approx}(x+b,q) = \mathrm{approx}(x,q)$ and
$\mathrm{approx}(-x,q) = \mathrm{approx}(x,q)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
$\nd{q(x+b)} = \nd{qx + qb} = \nd{qx}$ ($qb \in \ZZ$); and
$\nd{q(-x)} = \nd{-(qx)} = \nd{qx}$. Multiply by $q$.
\end{proof}

\section{Rigidity: exact invariance on the affine $\pm1$ subgroup}

\begin{theorem}[Translation invariance of $\Lc$]
For all $x \in \RR$, $b \in \ZZ$: $\Lc(x + b) = \Lc(x)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The sequences $q \mapsto \mathrm{approx}(x+b,q)$ and $q \mapsto \mathrm{approx}(x,q)$
are identical (Corollary 3.4); their $\liminf$s agree.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reflection invariance of $\Lc$]
For all $x \in \RR$: $\Lc(-x) = \Lc(x)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Identical, using the reflection half of Corollary 3.4.
\end{proof}

The maps $x \mapsto x+b$ ($b \in \ZZ$) and $x \mapsto -x$ are the affine
generators of determinant $\pm 1$.

\begin{corollary}[Spectrum on the affine $\pm1$ subgroup]
If $M$ is such an affine map (or composition), then $|\Delta| = 1$ and
$\Vspec(M) = \{1\} = [\,|\Delta|^{-1}, |\Delta|\,]$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
$\Lc(Mx) = \Lc(x)$ for every $x$ by Theorems 4.1--4.2, so every ratio is $1$; and
$[\,|\Delta|^{-1}, |\Delta|\,] = [1,1] = \{1\}$.
\end{proof}

These results are unconditional and exact (term-by-term). The extension to the
full modular group $\mathrm{GL}_2(\ZZ)$, requiring the inversion $x \mapsto -1/x$,
is conjectural (C3, \S8).

\section{Elasticity: the dilation lower bound}

For $|\Delta| > 1$ the constant deforms. The model case is a dilation $x \mapsto
nx$ ($n \ge 1$), matrix $\left(\begin{smallmatrix} n & 0 \\ 0 & 1\end{smallmatrix}
\right)$, $\Delta = n$.

\begin{lemma}[Subsequence bound; \texttt{Lc\_le\_liminf\_subseq}]
For every $x \in \RR$ and integer $q \ge 1$,
$\Lc(x) \le \liminf_{k \to \infty} \mathrm{approx}(x, qk)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A $\liminf$ over $\NN$ is bounded above by the $\liminf$ over any cofinal
subsequence, since the subsequence filter refines $\mathrm{atTop}$; in $\Rpos$ this
monotonicity is unconditional.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dilation lower bound; \texttt{Lc\_dilation\_lower}]
For every $x \in \RR$ and integer $n \ge 1$,
\[
\Lc(nx) \ge \frac1n\,\Lc(x), \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad
\frac{\Lc(nx)}{\Lc(x)} \ge \frac1n = |\Delta|^{-1}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
From $\mathrm{approx}(nx, k) = n k \,\nd{(nk)x} = \mathrm{approx}(x, nk)$, take
$\liminf_k$ and apply Lemma 5.1 to compare with the full sequence; the factor
$1/n$ emerges after the $\Rpos$ bookkeeping.
\end{proof}

The matching upper bound $\Lc(nx) \le n\Lc(x)$ is conjectural (C1, \S8).

\section{Bridge: badly approximable reals are irrational}

\begin{criterion}[Small-nonzero-form criterion]
If a real $x$ satisfies: for every $\varepsilon > 0$ there are $q \ge 1$,
$p \in \ZZ$ with $0 < |qx - p| < \varepsilon$, then $x$ is irrational. (Imported as
\texttt{EulerMascheroni.irrational\_of\_forall\_eps\_linear\_form}.)
\end{criterion}

\begin{theorem}[Nonvanishing along dilates; \texttt{ndist\_pos\_of\_bad}]
If $x \in \Bad$ and $q \ge 1$, then $qx \notin \ZZ$, i.e. $\nd{qx} > 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $\nd{qx} = 0$ then $qx = m \in \ZZ$ (Lemma 3.3), so $(qk)x = km \in \ZZ$ and
$\mathrm{approx}(x, qk) = 0$ for all $k$. Then
$\liminf_k \mathrm{approx}(x, qk) = 0$, and by Lemma 5.1, $\Lc(x) = 0$,
contradicting $x \in \Bad$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Small nonzero forms; \texttt{bad\_small\_forms}]
If $x \in \Bad$, then for every $\varepsilon > 0$ there are $q \ge 1$, $p \in \ZZ$
with $0 < |qx - p| < \varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Pick $n \ge 1$ with $1/(n+1) < \varepsilon$. By Dirichlet
(\texttt{Real.exists\_nat\_abs\_mul\_sub\_round\_le}) there is $q \ge 1$ with
$|qx - \round(qx)| \le 1/(n+1)$. Set $p = \round(qx)$; then $|qx - p| = \nd{qx} >
0$ (Theorem 6.2) and $|qx - p| \le 1/(n+1) < \varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bridge theorem; \texttt{irrational\_of\_bad}]
Every badly approximable real is irrational: $x \in \Bad \implies x \in \RR
\setminus \QQ$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem 6.3 and Criterion 6.1.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[\texttt{bad\_subset\_irrational}]
$\Bad \subseteq \{x : x \text{ irrational}\}$.
\end{corollary}

The inclusion is proper (Liouville numbers are irrational but not in $\Bad$) and
the proof is constructive.

\section{Toward full attainment}

Sections 4--6 establish (i) $\Vspec(M) = \{1\}$ for the affine $\pm1$ generators;
(ii) $\Lc(nx)/\Lc(x) \ge 1/n$ for dilations; (iii) non-vacuity of $\Bad$. The
remaining content of the Main Theorem is the attainment of every interior value, a
constructive statement. Writing $x = [a_0; a_1, a_2, \ldots]$, the best-approximation
denominators $q_j$ and local quality $q_j\nd{q_j x}$ are governed by the partial
quotients. The identity $\mathrm{approx}(nx, k) = \mathrm{approx}(x, nk)$ reduces
the dilation ratio to comparing the full sequence with its multiples-of-$n$
subsequence; alternating long runs of large and small partial quotients at
controlled scales realises any target $\rho \in [1/n, n]$. This is conjecture C2.
The general matrix reduces to the dilation case via the modular invariance of \S4
(C3) and isolation of the scalar part (C4).

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{C1. Upper dilation bound $\Lc(nx) \le n\Lc(x)$.}
For every real $x$ and $n \ge 1$. With Theorem 5.2 this pins the dilation ratio to
$[1/n, n]$. A three-distance/pigeonhole argument shows that among any $n$
consecutive best-approximation denominators, one lands in the progression
$q \equiv 0 \pmod n$ with comparable quality; the $\Rpos$ framework discharges the
boundedness conditions, leaving only the combinatorial AP estimate.

\paragraph{C2. The dilation ratio set is the full $[1/n, n]$.}
$\{\Lc(nx)/\Lc(x) : x \in \Bad\} = [1/n, n]$, every value attained, by designing
the continued fraction so the multiples-of-$n$ subsequence realises any prescribed
ratio against the full sequence.

\paragraph{C3. $\det = \pm1$ rigidity beyond the affine subgroup.}
For every $M \in \mathrm{GL}_2(\ZZ)$, $\Lc(Mx) = \Lc(x)$. The group is generated
by $x \mapsto x+1$ and $x \mapsto -1/x$; only the inversion (the CF shift
$[a_0;a_1,\ldots] \mapsto [a_1;a_2,\ldots]$) remains.

\paragraph{C4. Endpoint sharpness needs $\gcd(a,b,c,d) = 1$.}
Dropping primitivity strictly shrinks the attained set: for $M = k M_0$ the
spectrum collapses toward that of $M_0$, so $|\Delta|^{\pm1}$ are not attained. The
common factor $k$ acts as a pure dilation controlled by the C1/Theorem 5.2 bounds.

\section{Discussion and conclusion}

The dichotomy is clean: on the determinant-$\pm1$ modular world $\Lc$ is a hard
invariant (rigidity, $\Vspec = \{1\}$); for $|\Delta| > 1$ it is elastic within a
determinant-sized window that, by the Main Theorem, is filled completely. Badly
approximable frequencies govern the stability of quasiperiodic systems (KAM
theory), and $\nd{qx}$ is the basic quantity of sampling and lattice reduction;
understanding how $\Lc$ transforms under integer change of variables controls how
approximation quality propagates. We have established the verified foundation:
the $\Rpos$ formulation, exact affine rigidity, the dilation lower bound, and the
irrationality bridge; the Main Theorem follows together with the constructive
attainment program and conjectures C1--C4.

